            <ArticleTitle>Acute low back pain: diagnostics and treatment.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>80-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>How many times have you heard from a patient or groaned yourself &quot;Oh, my aching back?&quot; Innocuous movements such as reaching, stooping, or leaning are halted mid-performance as you sense &quot;something&quot; give, catch, snap, grab, or slide in your lower back. Such subjective complaints may also include sensations of discomfort described as stabbing, sharp, dull, hot/burning, tingling, or numbing. In practice, you will be required to assess these subjective symptoms, effectively document objective data, formulate a diagnosis, and plan appropriate treatment for your patients. Careful attention to history, associated symptoms, and following a systematic approach to back pain can make the rule-in/out differentials less taxing on both the practitioner and the patient.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
